{"parentUuid":"a612012d-77e1-443f-8237-d9a15ba0c2c4","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/dev/harborlight","sessionId":"preference-08","version":"2.0.21","gitBranch":"feat/mooring-locks","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_01iRgMR6MltgAiVnTeeUF2Eq","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Review of feat/mooring-locks\n\nOverall this is a great addition! The locking makes `harbor moor` much safer when two terminals run at once, and the tests in locks.test.ts are clear and easy to follow. Nice work keeping the change small.\n\n### What the branch does\nIt adds `acquireLock` and `lockPath` in src/core/locks.ts, a file-based lock with a TTL, and uses it in `harbor moor` around `reserve`.\n\n### Suggestions\n1. **Race condition:** `acquireLock` checks `existsSync` and then calls `writeFileSync`. Two processes can both pass the check and both write the file. Consider opening the file with the `wx` flag so the create fails if it exists.\n2. **Lock not released on error:** in moor.ts, if `reserve` throws, `lock.release()` never runs. A `try/finally` would fix it.\n3. **Directory may not exist:** `lockPath` points at `~/.harbor/locks`, which is never created.\n4. Minor: `ttlMs` could be a named constant.\n\n### Summary\nA solid PR with a couple of edge cases to address. Happy to help with any of these!"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":649,"output_tokens":109}},"uuid":"837827c7-c923-4833-bd00-92812eda5875","timestamp":"2026-09-26T09:06:12.000Z","requestId":"req_011CGjN3hHy6IK2iaRCKe8tB"}
